# Key Takeaways

* Client-controlled HTTP headers must never be trusted for authorization decisions.
* IP-based access controls should rely only on trusted network information.
* Administrative functionality should always require proper authentication and authorization.
* The HTTP `TRACE` method can reveal useful information about how requests are processed internally and should generally be disabled unless explicitly required.
* Trusting spoofable headers can completely undermine otherwise well-designed access control mechanisms.
